<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Lament · 1895 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Suliko</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ka">სულიკო</bdi> / <bdi lang="ka-Latn">Suliko</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Georgian love song from 1895 about searching for someone dear who is gone. It became known all over the Soviet Union.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Akaki Tsereteli</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Varinka Tsereteli</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Georgians; later sung throughout the Soviet Union</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Georgia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1895</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Akaki Tsereteli and music by Varinka Tsereteli (1895))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suliko">Suliko</a>&quot; is a love poem written in 1895 by the Georgian poet Akaki Tsereteli. Suliko is a Georgian name, given to both women and men, meaning &quot;soul&quot;. At the poet's request his relative Varinka Tsereteli set it to music in the same year, and in that form it became widely known throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> it was often played on the radio, reputedly because it was his favourite song. It was translated and sung in many languages, among them Russian, Ukrainian, Polish, Romanian, English, German, Basque, Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>.</p><p>Tsereteli was closely tied to the Georgian national liberation movement, and the song has been read as carrying his wish for an independent Georgian homeland. Scholars have given it other meanings too. Akaki Bakradze saw in the succession of the rose, the nightingale and the star a rising search for God and for the lost paradise from which mankind was exiled; Emzar Kvitaishvili likewise read the search for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suliko">Suliko</a> as the search for a supreme love and for God. Others have taken Suliko as a symbol of any distant, unreachable dream, whether a person's peace with himself or his place in life.</p><p>A folk story about its origin was recorded by the poet and folklorist Eter Tataraidze in the village of Toneti, in the Tetritskaro region. In it, Akaki dedicated the poem to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suliko">Suliko</a> Samadashvili of Toneti, the brother of a friend: he had gone to town to buy clothes for his wedding and was killed in the forest, and his remains were found only months later. When Akaki came to the grave he found Suliko's beloved weeping there, and so changed his first line, which had spoken of a brother's grave, into one about a beloved's. The story is folklore, not established history, but it shows how deeply the song entered Georgian popular belief.</p><p>The song is performed in many traditions, from Georgian ensembles such as Mdzlevari and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batumi</a> ensemble to the Alexandrov Ensemble and university choirs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and singers abroad.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A mourner searches in vain for the grave of a beloved, and the song passes through images of a rose, a nightingale and a star. The mood is gentle and sorrowful, a longing that many have read as a search for something beyond reach.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Georgian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Georgian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">საყვარლის საფლავს ვეძებდი,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Saq'varlis saplavs vedzebdi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I was looking for my beloved's grave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ვერ ვნახე!.. დაკარგულიყო!..</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Ver vnakhe!.. Dak'arguliq'o!..</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I could not find it!.. It had been lost!..</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">გულამოსკვნილი ვჩიოდი</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Gulamosk'vnili vchiodi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With my heart choked with sobs I lamented:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">„სადა ხარ, ჩემო სულიკო?!“</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">„Sada khar, chemo Sulik'o?!“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Where are you, my Suliko?!”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ეკალში ვარდი შევნიშნე,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Ek'alshi vardi shevnishne,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Among the thorns I noticed a rose</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ობლად რომ ამოსულიყო,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Oblad rom amosuliq'o,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That had grown up there all alone,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">გულის ფანცქალით ვკითხავდი</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Gulis pantskalit vk'itkhavdi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With a fluttering heart I asked:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">„შენ ხომ არა ხარ სულიკო?!“</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">„Shen khom ara khar Sulik'o?!“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Are you not Suliko?!”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ნიშნად თანხმობის კოკობი</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Nishnad tankhmobis k'ok'obi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As a sign of assent the bud</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">შეირხა... თავი დახარა,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Sheirkha... tavi dakhara,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Stirred... it bowed its head,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ცვარ-მარგალიტი ციური</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Tsvar-margalit'i tsiuri</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heavenly dew-pearls</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">დაბლა ცრემლებად დაჰყარა.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Dabla tsremlebad dahq'ara.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It scattered down as tears.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">სულგანაბული ბულბული</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Sulganabuli bulbuli</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holding its breath, a nightingale</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ფოთლებში მიმალულიყო,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Potlebshi mimaluliq'o,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Had hidden among the leaves,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">მივეხმატკბილე ჩიტუნას</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Mivekhmat'k'bile chit'unas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I spoke sweetly to the little bird:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">„შენ ხომ არა ხარ სულიკო?!“</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">„Shen khom ara khar Sulik'o?!“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Are you not Suliko?!”</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 48 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">შეიფრთქიალა მგოსანმა,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Sheiprtkiala mgosanma,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The singer fluttered its wings,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ყვავილს ნისკარტი შეახო,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Q'vavils nisk'art'i sheakho,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Touched the flower with its beak,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ჩაიკვნეს-ჩაიჭიკჭიკა,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Chaik'vnes-chaich'ik'ch'ik'a,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It sighed and twittered,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">თითქოს სთქვა „დიახ, დიახო!“</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Titkos stkva „diakh, diakho!“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As if it said: “Yes, yes!”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">დაგვქათქათებდა ვარსკვლავი,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Dagvkatkatebda varsk'vlavi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A star was shining white upon us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">სხივები გადმოსულიყო,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Skhivebi gadmosuliq'o,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Its rays had come down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">მას შევეკითხე შეფრქვევით</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Mas shevek'itkhe sheprkvevit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I asked it in a rush:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">„შენ ხომ არა ხარ სულიკო?!“</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">„Shen khom ara khar Sulik'o?!“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Are you not Suliko?!”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">დასტური მომცა ციმციმით,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Dast'uri momtsa tsimtsimit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It gave me its assent by twinkling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">სხივები გადმომაყარა</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Skhivebi gadmomaq'ara</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It poured its rays down on me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">და იმ დროს ყურში ჩურჩულით</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Da im dros q'urshi churchulit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And at that moment, whispering in my ear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ნიავმაც ასე მახარა</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Niavmats ase makhara</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The breeze too brought me this good news:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">„ეგ არის, რასაც ეძებდი,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">„Eg aris, rasats edzebdi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“That is the one you were looking for,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">მორჩი და მოისვენეო!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Morchi da moisveneo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have done, and rest!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">დღე დაიღამე აწ ტკბილად</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Dghe daighame ats' t'k'bilad</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bring your day to evening sweetly now</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">და ღამე გაითენეო!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Da ghame gaiteneo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And bring your night to dawn!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">„სამად შექმნილა ის ერთი</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">„Samad shekmnila is erti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“That one has been made into three:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ვარსკვლავად, ბულბულ, ვარდადო,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Varsk'vlavad, bulbul, vardado,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A star, a nightingale, a rose,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">თქვენ ერთმანეთი რადგანაც</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Tkven ertmaneti radganats</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since you each other</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ამ ქვეყნად შეგიყვარდათო“.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Am kveq'nad shegiq'vardato“.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Came to love in this world.”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">მენიშნა!.. აღარ დავეძებ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Menishna!.. Aghar davedzeb</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I understood the sign!.. No longer do I seek</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">საყვარლის კუბო-სამარეს,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Saq'varlis k'ubo-samares,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My beloved's coffin and grave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">აღარც შევჩივი ქვეყანას,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Agharts shevchivi kveq'anas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No longer do I complain to the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">აღარ ვღვრი ცრემლებს მდუღარეს!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Aghar vghvri tsremlebs mdughares!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No longer do I shed scalding tears!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ბულბულს ყურს ვუგდებ, ვარდს ვყნოსავ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Bulbuls q'urs vugdeb, vards vq'nosav,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I listen to the nightingale, I breathe in the rose,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ვარსკვლავს შევყურებ ლხენითა</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Varsk'vlavs shevq'ureb lkhenita</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I gaze at the star with joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">და, რასაცა ვგრძნობ მე იმ დროს,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Da, rasatsa vgrdznob me im dros,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And what I feel at that time</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ვერ გამომითქვამს ენითა!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Ver gamomitkvams enita!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I cannot put into words with my tongue!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ისევ გამეხსნა სიცოცხლე,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Isev gamekhsna sitsotskhle,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Life opened up for me again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">დღემდე რომ მწარედ კრულ იყო,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Dghemde rom mts'ared k'rul iq'o,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Which until this day had been bitterly cursed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">ახლა კი ვიცი, სადაც ხარ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Akhla k'i vitsi, sadats khar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And now I know where you are:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ka">სამგან გაქვს ბინა, სულიკო!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ka-Latn">Samgan gakvs bina, Sulik'o!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You have your dwelling in three places, Suliko!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Akaki Tsereteli (1895); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Georgian Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://ka.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E1%83%A1%E1%83%A3%E1%83%9A%E1%83%98%E1%83%99%E1%83%9D_(%E1%83%A1%E1%83%98%E1%83%9B%E1%83%A6%E1%83%94%E1%83%A0%E1%83%90)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Georgian national system).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: the Georgian script has no capitals; the first word of a line and the name Sulik'o are capitalized here.</li><li>Suliko is a name of endearment, from 'suli', soul.</li><li>line 3.3: 'ცვარ-მარგალიტი', literally 'dew-pearl', pearls of dew.</li><li>line 5.1: 'მგოსანი' is a poet or singer, here the nightingale.</li><li>line 5.3: 'ჩაიკვნესა' is to moan or sigh softly; 'ჩაიჭიკჭიკა' to twitter.</li><li>line 6.1: 'დაგვქათქათებდა' is 'was gleaming white upon us'.</li><li>line 6.3: 'შეფრქვევით' [?], literally 'with a sprinkling, a gush'; read here as 'in a rush (of feeling)'.</li><li>lines 8.2, 8.4, 9.2, 9.4: the ending -o marks reported speech (the breeze's words).</li><li>line 8.3-8.4: 'spend the day into evening ... spend the night until dawn', i.e. live your days and nights in peace.</li></ul></details></section>
